{"success": true, "name": "arc-task-delta-inference", "file": "references/compact-output-reassessment-after-errors.md", "content": "# Compact-output reassessment after incorrect submissions

When inputs are partitioned by repeated separator rows or columns but demos produce much smaller outputs, dimension ratios help identify possible structure but do not establish the encoding. Keep distinct hypotheses for (a) summarizing interior regions, (b) recording separator/boundary markers at their intersections, and (c) selecting only rows or columns with special markers. For each demo, write down the exact partition counts and map candidate input coordinates or blocks to each output cell, including ordering. Reproduce every output exactly before transferring the map.

After an incorrect compact-output submission, do not merely alter colors or copy another visually salient set of markers. Reopen the task class and output representation, and re-derive dimensions and correspondence from all demos. Repeated, heterogeneous demos may share only an abstract structure; spend another demo only when two explicit mappings predict a materially different discriminating observation.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u005/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-task-delta-inference/references/compact-output-reassessment-after-errors.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/compact-region-mapping.md", "content": "# Compact region-to-output mapping

When a partitioned input has a much smaller output, investigate a region summary as a live task class instead of assuming a same-size edit or stamp. Identify separator rows/columns, compute the number and arrangement of regions, and associate each region with the corresponding output cell. Inspect marker presence, marker color/identity, count, and position within each region as candidate encodings. Verify the exact output—including default values for empty regions—against every demonstration before transfer. Output dimensions alone do not establish the mapping. Before spending another demo request, list at least two candidate encodings and state what observation in the next pair would distinguish them.

In a demo with sparse markers embedded in a regular partitioned canvas, the compact output preserved a 6×3 region arrangement and represented localized marker colors at corresponding output positions; most empty positions retained the canvas color. Treat this as a diagnostic pattern, not a universal rule: confirm separators and correspondence in the current task.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u005/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/compact-region-mapping.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-evidence-recovery", "file": "references/task-217fc4ad-review.md", "content": "# Session review: premature speculative submissions after multiple demos

This instance supplied six substantially different input/output examples, generally with a full-width or full-height divider and outputs that retained one side/panel in reduced form. No shared deterministic encoding was established by the demos. The test was a 9×2 grid containing 5s and a 7, but repeated guesses alternated among one-column, two-column recolorings, and all-zero outputs; an identical one-column guess was even submitted twice. Those candidates were not supported by exact replay against the demonstrations and all failed.

Reusable recovery lesson: after every rejection, record the exact candidate grid and its hypothesis before acting. Immediately verify the next proposed action is materially different and has evidence; if it is not, stop. Never let attempt-budget pressure or an unresolved task justify arbitrary candidates. On a demo turn, first identify what the new pair adds to the common rule; only request again for a stated discriminator between live hypotheses. This case remained unresolved, so it provides a process warning, not a transformation rule.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u005/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-evidence-recovery/references/task-217fc4ad-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-evidence-recovery", "file": "references/task-29b1b5eb-instance-review.md", "content": "# Task 29b1b5eb: fresh-instance evidence caution

This review involved a new stream instance bearing a task ID that had appeared in historical skill references. The prior note suggested rightward extensions but explicitly warned it was not a validated universal rule. Transferring that hypothesis directly led to unvalidated output; repeated requests were made without an explicit discriminator or a cumulative pair-by-pair delta ledger. The resulting grid submission was reported as an invalid ARC grid, and the attempted retry repeated the same malformed shape.

A fresh task instance requires independent evidence. Treat same-ID references as historical warnings, not transformation rules. For every returned pair, immediately record exact changed coordinates and unaffected candidates, update named hypotheses, and request again only for an observation that separates plausible rules. When invalid-grid feedback arrives, check row count, widths, dimensions, values, and action serialization before making another attempt; keep format diagnosis separate from inference. This session did not establish a reliable transformation for its test, so this reference documents process failures and recovery gates, not a solved output rule.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u005/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-evidence-recovery/references/task-29b1b5eb-instance-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/periodic-field-inference.md", "content": "# Periodic-field hypothesis from heterogeneous demos

When a demo appears unrelated to another by palette, dimensions, or local geometry, first inspect whether each input nevertheless encodes an ordered sequence or lattice that is expanded into a full periodic output. For a periodic candidate, independently derive the period, symbol/color order, direction, and phase/origin from the input markers and output. Verify the complete generated grid against every demo, including row/column transitions; a visually plausible periodic fill is not evidence by itself. Heterogeneity alone is not grounds either to dismiss a shared abstract rule or to repeatedly request demonstrations without a discriminating question.

This is a diagnostic technique, not a solved task-specific mapping. In the reviewed stream, the demos included sparse color tracks and an already periodic output; the test likewise contained diagonal color tracks, but no transfer rule was rigorously established before the next demo request. Future analyses should extract and validate the proposed sequence-to-field mapping before submission.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u005/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/periodic-field-inference.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/panel-count-encoding.md", "content": "# Panel marker-count encoding: diagnostic and validation lesson

This instance had grids partitioned by full separator bands and compact outputs shaped like the panel array. Several demos were consistent with an output marker for panels containing multiple instances of the designated non-background color; others had different marker colors and output backgrounds. A test answer based on a guessed threshold and manually counted panels was rejected, so the evidence does not establish the exact test encoding or make a reusable color/threshold rule.

Reusable method: transcribe the grid exactly; locate separator rows/columns; define half-open panel bounds; count target-color cells in every panel; build a table of panel coordinates, counts, and corresponding output values. Test competing count mappings (including exact count versus threshold) against *all* demonstrations, especially panels with zero, one, two, and three-or-more markers. Confirm orientation, output dimensions, background encoding, and values separately. After rejection, first independently re-check serialization and the panel count table; do not repeat a merely reformatted candidate. Do not claim a count rule until full demo replay supports it.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u005/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/panel-count-encoding.md"}
